    "source": "// `verified effect` — inverse round-trip testing (roadmap item 26,\n// docs/verified-effect.md, syntax-2.0 §7).\n//\n// The checker proves an inverse is *present and well-shaped* (G4), never that\n// it is *correct* — \"an undo that is wrong, partial, or a no-op\" is the gap\n// docs/replay.md §4.1 names outright. A `verified effect` opts an\n// activation-body effect into a test nobody wrote: `revl test` activates the\n// component, tears it down so the inverse runs, and asserts the observable\n// in-process state returned to baseline — N randomized rounds, on the real\n// py runtime. Not a proof; in-process state only (aliases, external effects\n// and clock values are out of reach). See docs/verified-effect.md.\n\nservice Store {\n  fn seed(k: Str) -> Int\n  fn unseed(k: Str) -> Int\n}\n\nservice Ready {\n  fn ok() -> Str\n}\n\ncomponent Backing provides s: Store {\n  let data = effect Map.new() undo data.drop()\n\n  provide s {\n    fn seed(k)   = data.insert(k, 1)\n    fn unseed(k) = data.remove(k)\n  }\n}\n\n// Seeder's activation seeds a key into the shared store; its inverse unseeds\n// it. `verified` marks the pair for round-trip testing. `config.tag` is the\n// randomized input surface — every round activates with a fresh tag.\ncomponent Seeder requires s: Store provides r: Ready {\n  config { tag: Str }\n\n  let hold = verified effect s.seed(config.tag) undo s.unseed(config.tag)\n\n  provide r {\n    fn ok() = \"ok\"\n  }\n}\n"
