    "id": "lifecycle_timer",
    "label": "timers under test — advance the clock, assert firings",
    "kind": "accept",
    "file": "lifecycle_timer.rvl",
    "source": "// Timers under `revl test` (docs/time-coeffect.md, roadmap items 57 + 102).\n//\n// Item 57 made a timer a *revertible schedule* whose inverse is cancellation,\n// and the clock a *coeffect the harness provides* — a firing is a deterministic\n// timeline step, never a wall-clock race. Item 57 proved the first half inside\n// a `lifecycle test` (unload cancels, residue-free), but the second half — a\n// firing — was not expressible in the language, because nothing in a lifecycle\n// test could move the clock.\n//\n// Item 102 closes that: the `advance <n><unit>` lifecycle statement drives the\n// same clock coeffect forward, so a timer's firing becomes an assertable step.\n// A `TickCounter` records one entry per firing, so `count()` reads back exactly\n// how many times a timer's body ran — \"fires on the 3rd tick\", in-language.\n\nservice Counter {\n  fn count() -> Int\n  emission fn tick()\n}\n\ncomponent TickCounter provides counter: Counter {\n  let store = effect Map.new() undo store.drop()\n\n  provide counter {\n    fn count() = store.size()\n    fn tick() {\n      // one distinct entry per firing; `count()` is `store.size()`, so an\n      // advance that fires the body N times leaves count == N.\n      let key = `tick-${store.size()}`\n      effect store.insert(key, \"fired\")\n      undo   store.remove(key)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\ncomponent Heartbeat requires counter: Counter {\n  every 10s { emit counter.tick() }\n}\n\ncomponent Warmup requires counter: Counter {\n  after 50s { emit counter.tick() }\n}\n\n// `every`: the body runs on each tick the advance crosses, and cancellation on\n// unload is residue-free — a later advance produces no orphaned firing.\nlifecycle test \"an every-timer fires on each advanced tick\" {\n  load TickCounter\n  load Heartbeat\n  let before = call counter.count()\n  assert before == 0                 // the clock never moves on its own\n  advance 35s\n  let ticks = call counter.count()\n  assert ticks == 3                  // fired at 10s, 20s, 30s — the 3rd tick\n  unload Heartbeat                    // cancellation is the schedule's inverse\n  advance 100s\n  let settled = call counter.count()\n  assert settled == 3                // no orphaned firing after teardown\n  unload TickCounter\n  assert no_residue\n}\n\n// `after`: a one-shot fires exactly once, only after its delay elapses, and is\n// then spent — advancing further never fires it again.\nlifecycle test \"an after-timer fires once when its delay elapses\" {\n  load TickCounter\n  load Warmup\n  advance 30s\n  let early = call counter.count()\n  assert early == 0                  // 30s < 50s — not yet\n  advance 30s\n  let fired = call counter.count()\n  assert fired == 1                  // crossed 50s — fired once\n  advance 100s\n  let spent = call counter.count()\n  assert spent == 1                  // one-shot is spent — never fires again\n  unload Warmup\n  unload TickCounter\n  assert no_residue\n}\n"
