    "id": "handoff_cache",
    "label": "state handoff — a hot-swap that keeps the cache warm",
    "kind": "accept",
    "file": "handoff_cache.rvl",
    "source": "// Verified state hand-off on hot-swap (roadmap item 53, docs/state-handoff.md).\n//\n// `handoff cache: Map[Str, Str]` declares the SHAPE of this provider's live\n// state, and it reads both ways: when this component is *replaced*, that type\n// is the value it exports; when it is the *replacement*, that is the shape it\n// accepts. At swap admission the §5 compatibility relation is pointed at state\n// instead of interface — a successor whose declared shape cannot hold the\n// predecessor's is refused with a why-trace (`why.kind ==\n// \"state-handoff-drift\"`), because silently dropping live state on a swap\n// would be residue (G7).\n//\n// A component declares at most one `handoff`, as a prelude declaration, on a\n// key it provides. A component with no `handoff` lowers byte-identically to\n// before; in the IR the declaration is `component[\"handoff\"]`.\n\nservice Store {\n  fn get(k: Str) -> Opt[Str]\n  fn put(k: Str, v: Str)\n}\n\ncomponent Cache provides cache: Store {\n  handoff cache: Map[Str, Str]           // the state contract\n  let m = effect Map.new() undo m.drop() // the state itself\n\n  provide cache {\n    fn get(k) = m.get(k)\n    fn put(k, v) {\n      effect m.insert(k, v)\n      undo   m.remove(k)\n    }\n  }\n}\n"
